course: dictionary
lesson: 10553
title: Unbelievable: wow or not true?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unbelievable has more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one. «And some true facts sound unbelievable» means they are so surprising that they are hard to believe, even though they are true. The clue is the word true.
explain: When someone says «That's unbelievable!» about a brilliant goal or a huge slice of cake, they mean it is so good or so great that it is hard to take in. This is informal talk, so it shows strong feeling. Ask yourself: is the writer surprised and impressed, or saying the thing may be false?
explain: «A tall tale is an invented, unbelievable story.» The clue is invented: the story is made up, so unbelievable means it is not likely to be true. Check by swapping in your meaning: «an invented story that is not likely to be true» still makes sense.
fact: Unbelievable means so surprising or strange that it is hard to believe it is true.
fact: In informal talk, unbelievable can mean very good, very bad or very great.
fact: An unbelievable story is one that is not likely to be true.
fact: Unbelievably is the adverb, as in «unbelievably lucky»: it tells you how much.
fact: Careful: something can be unbelievable and still be true, as in «some true facts sound unbelievable».
exercise: «And some true facts sound unbelievable.» What does unbelievable mean here? | Completely false and invented | * So surprising it is hard to believe, even though it is true | Boring and dull | Very cheap and easy to buy
exercise: Ben says, «That goal was unbelievable!» What does he most likely mean? | The goal was very small | The goal was a trick | The goal was not allowed by the referee | * The goal was amazing and very great
exercise: «A tall tale is an invented, unbelievable story.» Find and copy the word that tells you the story is made up. | * invented | tall | story | tale
test:
A website offers a brand-new car for one pound. The offer seems unbelievable. What might it be? | Real, and the car will definitely be sent to you | * Too good to be true, so it may be a trick | A very small, boring offer | A story from a library book
«She was unbelievably lucky to find the lost ring.» What does unbelievably mean here? | Very slowly | * So much that it is hard to believe | Quietly and sadly | Not at all
exam: Which sentence uses unbelievable to mean a story that is probably not true? | * Her tale of flying to the moon was unbelievable. | The lunch bell rang at noon. | The river was wide and cold. | We ate pizza for lunch.
